  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on self-reported purchase intentions rather than actual purchasing behavior.",
      "what": "This study analyzed the factors driving New Energy Vehicle (NEV) purchase intentions among China's Generation Z using survey data from 487 young adults. The researchers integrated the Technology Acceptance Model, the Theory of Planned Behaviour, and Innovation Diffusion Theory, analyzing the data through Partial Least Squares Structural Equation Modelling. The model revealed that perceived usefulness, environmental consciousness, compatibility, charging infrastructure, and smart networked technology positively influence purchase intentions, while perceived risk is not a significant barrier.",
      "why": "It identifies the specific drivers of electric vehicle adoption for a rising demographic in the world's largest NEV market, demonstrating that digital connectivity and infrastructure availability outweigh perceived risks for younger buyers."
    }
